# C, the coupling incidence, projected to what an `association` can carry. One row per
# (filing, from_layer, to_layer): this filing observed that relieving one layer moves the other.
#
# The model's own falsifier, drawn
#   `pm:Coupling` exists *so that the model can be refuted in its own format*: a stack is assumed
#   to be independent quantizations, and a coupling is the observation that says it is not. A
#   diagram silent about it is the artifact a reader is most likely to be shown and the one
#   incapable of carrying the refutation, which is what this relation is here to prevent.
#
# It is directed
#   That was checked rather than assumed. `composition/attenuated.sqlc` matches from to from and
#   to to to, `entries/spillovers.sqlc` joins each end separately, and
#   `checks/coupling_does_not_attenuate.sqlc` formats its own detail as `%s->%s`. The symmetric
#   falsifier is a different relation: `checks/layers_move_together.sqlc` reads
#   `rank/co_moving_layers.sqlc`, which is the descent intersected with its converse, so it is
#   about F and never about C. Two rules, two graphs, one English phrase.
#
# Incidence only, observation included
#   Here that refuses the `observation` as well as the strength. The observation is required prose
#   and it is the whole evidence, and some observations in this corpus contain a numeral: one
#   quotes `[0.10, 0.22, 0.35]` outright. Carrying it into a `documentation` would put a magnitude
#   on a page that has no unit anywhere, which is exactly the refusal `dataObject` carries,
#   arriving through prose instead of through a field.
#
# What the artifact cannot say
#   So the artifact says that two layers are coupled and cannot say what was seen. That is a
#   `loses`, and a wide one. It is still the first time the fact appears at all.
#
# Defined, not read
#   A program reads these columns, so this statement defines its vocabulary in its own
#   WITH clause: a view keeps the rows and loses the column's origin, which is where
#   NOT NULL lives. assets/sqlc/views.sqlc says what a definition is.
#
-- pm:Stack/pm:Coupling projected to its two ends; BPMN 2.0 tAssociation sourceRef/targetRef.
WITH
couplings AS NOT MATERIALIZED (
    :define(entries/couplings.sqlc)
)
SELECT c.filing, c.from_layer, c.to_layer
FROM (
    :compose(entries/couplings.sqlc)
) c
